# How to reproduce the issue

I ran into the issue while testing my company's product (identity/application aware proxies) beta support for Kubernetes. Trying that out is probably the easiest/fastest way for you to reproduce the issue. (I swear I'm not trying to promote it here lol).

It's free for personal use cases and really fast to get going, signup here https://www.border0.com/. Here's a video I made for a customer that might be helpful to you if you do give it a shot: https://drive.google.com/file/d/1I0xmkJ-4ybuzObABt69e11O8m-NVwF4F/view

`kubectl exec` with `kubectl` < v1.30 should work without any changes but should only work with the env `KUBECTL_REMOTE_COMMAND_WEBSOCKETS=false` for `kubectl` >= v1.30

I assume other authenticated "proxy as a service" services out there also use HTTPS proxies for Kubernetes support (are people still really using basic-authenticated proxies over HTTP in 2024? ...yes they are...)

# How to set up an HTTPS proxy in go:

On a high level to set up an HTTPS proxy in go:

- generate a self-signed cert / key-pair to use for TLS
  - [mkcert](https://github.com/FiloSottile/mkcert) automates adding the self-signed CA to your machine's trust-store
- set up an `http.Server` object and call `ListenAndServeTLS(/* args */)` on it

<details>

<summary>pseudocode</summary>

```
server := &http.Server{
    Addr:  "localhost:8443",
    Handler: handler,
    TLSConfig: &tls.Config{
	// Must force HTTP/1.1 to be used since HTTP/2.0
        // does not support the CONNECT HTTP method.
        NextProtos: []string{"http/1.1"}
    },
}

if err := server.ListenAndServeTLS("/path/to/your/cert.pem", "/path/to/your/key.pem"); err != nil {
    return fmt.Errorf("failed to listen and serve tls: %v", err)
}
```

</details>

- the handler in the server above (`http.Handler`) should handle the HTTP CONNECT method and set up a full duplex connection between the client and the remote server

<details>

<summary>pseudocode</summary>

```
var handler = http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
     if r.Method != http.MethodConnect {
        http.Error(w, fmt.Sprintf("only %s is allowed, but got %s", http.MethodConnect, r.Method), http.StatusMethodNotAllowed)
        return
    }
    tunnelToDestination(r.Host, cfg.TunnelDialTimeout, w)
})

// tunnelToDestination connects to a remote server and sets up a full
// duplex connection between the client and the remote server
func tunnelToDestination(
	targetAddress string,
	dialTimeout time.Duration,
	w http.ResponseWriter,
) {
	toDestination, err := net.DialTimeout("tcp", targetAddress, dialTimeout)
	if err != nil {
                 // probably log or something
		http.Error(w, "an unknown error occurred... try again later", http.StatusServiceUnavailable)
		return
	}

	w.WriteHeader(http.StatusOK)

	hijacker, ok := w.(http.Hijacker)
	if !ok {
                 // probably log or something
		http.Error(w, "an unknown error occurred... try again later", http.StatusInternalServerError)
		return
	}

	toClient, _, err := hijacker.Hijack()
	if err != nil {
                 // probably log or something
		http.Error(w, "an unknown error occurred... try again later", http.StatusServiceUnavailable)
		return
	}

	if err = iocopy(toClient, toDestination); err != nil {
                 // probably log or something
		http.Error(w, "an unknown error occurred... try again later", http.StatusServiceUnavailable)
		return
	}
}

func iocopy(src, dst net.Conn) error {
	defer src.Close()
	defer dst.Close()

	errC := make(chan error, 2)
	defer close(errC)

	var wg sync.WaitGroup
	defer wg.Wait()

	wg.Add(1)
	go func() {
		defer wg.Done()

		// abort operations on dst when src has an error (incl. EOF)
		defer dst.SetDeadline(time.Now().Add(-1 * time.Hour))

		_, err := io.Copy(dst, src)
		errC <- err
	}()

	wg.Add(1)
	go func() {
		defer wg.Done()

		// abort operations on src when dst has an error (incl. EOF)
		defer src.SetDeadline(time.Now().Add(-1 * time.Hour))

		_, err := io.Copy(src, dst)
		errC <- err
	}()

	// returns the first error encountered
	return <-errC
}
```

